# Issue #1021 — the discriminating pair for `-`, the one binary operator whose two meanings share a
# character. Both spellings LEX identically, as `op(-)` followed by `number(1)`; token kind
# therefore cannot separate them and ADJACENCY does, exactly as grammar.md:60
# (`number ::= [ "-" ] digit …`) and grammar.md:230 ("A leading `-` on a numeral is part of a
# negative literal, not a unary operator") require.
#
# So glued `-1` is a negative ARGUMENT and keeps its parenthesized call, while spaced `- 1`
# continues the expression and makes the group a parenthesized expression. Nothing consults arity to
# tell them apart, which is what keeps the reader profile-blind — and it is why `-` needed no
# exception carved around it while the other nine operators were fixed. Executed because the two
# readings both parse clean: only the VALUES distinguish them, so a parse-only fixture would pass
# just as happily if the two were swapped.
define six
  return 6
end
print (round -1)
print (six - 1)
